## 8. Limitations

- **Synthetic data only:** This study uses synthetically generated data for 3 Delhi monitoring stations over a 3-year period. Real-world performance may differ.
- **Single city:** The model is trained on Delhi data; direct generalization to other cities is not validated.
- **Mode B optimism:** Mode B results use observed future weather as a proxy for forecasts. Real weather forecasts introduce additional error.
- **Static models:** Models are trained once; seasonal drift or sensor calibration changes are not handled automatically.
- **AQI approximation:** The Indian AQI calculation requires at least 3 pollutants including PM2.5 or PM10; in periods where fewer pollutants are available, AQI may be missing.
- **No deep learning:** LSTM, Transformer, or other sequence models are listed as future work and not evaluated here.

---

## 9. Future Work

- **Gradient boosting:** Evaluate `HistGradientBoostingRegressor` and XGBoost/LightGBM for potentially better accuracy.
- **SARIMAX:** Time-series modelling with exogenous weather variables for one station.
- **Deep learning:** LSTM and Transformer architectures for sequence modelling.
- **Satellite data:** Integrate MODIS/VIIRS AOD (Aerosol Optical Depth) as an additional predictor.
- **Multi-city generalization:** Transfer learning or city-agnostic features for pan-India deployment.
- **Live data pipeline:** Automatic daily refresh from CPCB/OpenAQ and Open-Meteo forecast API.
- **Probabilistic forecasts:** Quantile regression or conformal prediction for uncertainty intervals.
- **Mobile app:** Native iOS/Android app consuming the FastAPI backend.

